  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Introduction\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU)** cache is a classic data structure that evicts the item that has not been accessed for the longest period when the cache reaches its capacity. This tutorial implements an LRU cache using Python's `collections.OrderedDict`, which maintains insertion order and provides O(1) operations for moving keys to the end.\n\n## Key Properties of `OrderedDict`\n\n`OrderedDict` inherits from `dict` but guarantees:\n1. **Ordering is preserved** \u2014 elements are stored in the order they were inserted.\n2. **O(1) move_to_end** \u2014 we can move an existing key to the end of the dictionary in constant time.\n3. **O(1) popitem(last=False)** \u2014 we can remove the first (oldest) item in constant time.\n\nThese properties make `OrderedDict` ideal for implementing LRU semantics without needing a doubly linked list.\n\n## Complete Implementation\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded LRU cache using OrderedDict.\n    \n    - Most recently accessed items stay at the end of the OrderedDict.\n    - Least recently used items are at the front and are evicted when capacity is exceeded.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the LRU cache with a given capacity.\n        \n        Args:\n            capacity: Maximum number of items the cache can hold. Must be > 0.\n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If capacity is less than or equal to 0.\n        \"\"\"\n        if capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be a positive integer.\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key) -> \"int | None\":\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value associated with the key.\n        \n        If the key exists:\n        - Move it to the end of the OrderedDict (mark as most recently used).\n        - Return its value.\n        If the key does not exist:\n        - Return -1 (or None, depending on convention; here we use -1 as in LeetCode style,\n          but return None is also common \u2014 we'll use -1 for clarity in tests).\n        \n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n            \n       ",
